WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.370
“Have you heard about all the stuff going
around?”

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:14.550
“I have.”

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:22.270
“Back in the day, your father and I would
handle this and if he was here now, maybe

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.760
we’d still handle it.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:29.120
But he’s not and that’s on you.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.449
So the way I see it, you gotta handle this.”

00:00:33.449 --> 00:00:37.300
Bringing the DIY spirit of punk and hardcore
music to indie filmmaking.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.300
How’s it going?

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.410
My name is Matt Wells.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.300
I’m an actor and producer on the independent
feature film CROWN &amp; ANCHOR, which will be

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.329
released theatrically in the fall of 2018.

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:52.460
CROWN &amp; ANCHOR is an intense family drama
which examines the effects of childhood trauma

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:56.329
and it is scored entirely with punk and hardcore
music.

00:00:56.329 --> 00:01:02.039
Bands like GORILLA BISCUITS, YOUTH OF TODAY,
DYS, JOHNNY THUNDERS, X-RAY SPEX (just to

00:01:02.039 --> 00:01:03.039
name a few).

00:01:03.039 --> 00:01:07.500
I made the movie in my town of St. John’s,
Newfoundland which is where I am right now

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.610
hiding from the rain under the trees.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:17.619
I made the movie with my best friend Michael
Rowe and I met him when I was in a band.

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:22.210
We were in a band together and that’s how
my journey started into filmmaking, it started

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.210
with music.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:27.740
We started to have some success on music television
in Canada.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:32.610
We were touring with bands like SLAYER and
FISHBONE and D.O.A.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:37.259
Eventually that journey led me into music
television.

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:43.959
So I all the sudden I went from being a singer
in this independent band to interviewing some

00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:46.260
of the bands that I admired.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:53.450
I became a music and film journalist on music
television and through the years I got to

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:59.959
interview people like Lou Reed and NO DOUBT,
Greta Gerwig and Quentin Tarantino.

00:01:59.959 --> 00:02:05.979
But somewhere along that journey I started
to feel I wanted to do something different

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:08.160
which was acting and filmmaking.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:14.069
And you can imagine what an aspiring actor
and filmmaker can learn from interviewing

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:15.260
people in that world.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:21.470
I did thousands of interviews and it was always
sort of percolating in my brain.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:28.129
To be quite honest with you what I learned
as a guy in an independent band, that’s

00:02:28.129 --> 00:02:31.890
what gave me the tools to be able to make
a movie.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.439
This sort of D.I.Y.

00:02:33.439 --> 00:02:40.180
(do-it-yourself) spirit of punk and hardcore
music, that’s what I lived, that was my

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:41.180
whole life.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:46.680
We were putting up our own posters, we were
raising money to make albums and music videos,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:52.240
running it like a small business so we could
go on tour and sustain ourselves to continue

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:59.180
doing something that we loved and that is
exactly how we made this movie and exactly

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.632
how we’re approaching the roll out of distribution.

00:03:00.632 --> 00:03:06.540
And when you think about it, those worlds
(independent film and independent music) that

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.930
punk and hardcore do-it-yourself culture and
spirit, they are very similar and that is

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:16.401
exactly the tools that we used to make CROWN
AND ANCHOR.

00:03:16.401 --> 00:03:18.010
What made the production of CROWN AND ANCHOR
unique?

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:27.299
Matt Wells: Our first day on set of CROWN
AND ANCHOR we shot 15 pages which was already

00:03:27.299 --> 00:03:32.569
longer than any project our writer/director
Andrew Rowe had worked on in the past which

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:35.430
sort of set the tone for the next 15 days
of filming.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:42.110
This was Andrew’s first feature and we only
had 15 days to make it happen.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:50.480
But it created an urgency on set which absolutely
you can see in the performances, you can see

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.520
in the direction.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:59.359
There is something about being under the gun
and knowing that you really don’t have the

00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:05.219
option to make a lot of mistakes or second-guess
yourself and that’s really what we did.

00:04:05.219 --> 00:04:12.430
And the backstory of CROWN AND ANCHOR is that
we had private investors and we had a strong

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:15.609
script, but it was long.

00:04:15.609 --> 00:04:18.209
And everybody told us it was too long at our
budget.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:22.490
We didn’t want to have to cut it because
of budget.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:26.199
Because we knew if we cut it at the script
phase, it was going to cut the heart out of

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:27.919
the film.

00:04:27.919 --> 00:04:33.270
And Andrew had a distinct vision for this
movie about wanting to really focus on character

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:39.560
development which was going to mean a lot
of long, one-take, no cut scenes.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:44.449
And Michael and I we connected with that vision
(we believed in it).

00:04:44.449 --> 00:04:50.750
So we wanted the ability to cut in the editing
room and not on the page.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:57.150
So we knew we had to assemble a team who was
amendable to that type of environment.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:01.690
I mean essentially we had to find people who
wanted a punk rock the heck out of this thing

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:04.490
and that’s what we did.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.810
We found an amazing cast, an amazing crew.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:11.050
I mean the good thing about having a very
strong script is that it allowed us to attract

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:16.240
very strong actors who had been in the business
for a very long time and without that sort

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.960
of experience, never would we be able to make
something like this.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.330
This was a no-frills set.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:28.020
There were no dressing rooms, no trailers,
it was stripped down.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.669
We had to have a team that were checking their
egos at the door and were cool to work in

00:05:31.669 --> 00:05:36.020
that sort of environment and just try to put
all the money on the screen, which is what

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:37.699
we did.

00:05:37.699 --> 00:05:42.009
Without this incredible cast and crew it never
would have happened.

00:05:42.009 --> 00:05:51.919
So that really, it influenced and it was a
big part of the energy you see on screen.

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:59.601
But not just the environment of production
but also the location.

00:05:59.601 --> 00:06:03.810
You know our creative team are spread all
over right now from Toronto to California,

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:08.520
but out home town is St. John’s, Newfoundland
(where I am right now) and there is something

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.419
really special about this place.

00:06:11.419 --> 00:06:16.500
And if you have a project I highly, highly
recommend you consider coming to make it here

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:21.009
not just because it has a growing and thriving
film community.

00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:28.870
Not just because it has an amazing crew here,
really talented actors and not just because

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:36.870
it’s beautiful but because there is an energy
in this place, it’s an island in the middle

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.569
of the North Atlantic Ocean.

00:06:38.569 --> 00:06:48.520
We are geographically isolated, we are socially
and culturally unique and it also informs

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:49.520
our movie.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:53.389
And if you don’t believe me how special
it is here just see if you can ask Jason Momoa

00:06:53.389 --> 00:06:54.770
I’m sure you can just call him.

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:56.830
The guy is here all the time filming.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:00.230
It’s not a rare occurrence to see him walking
down the street going to grab a coffee, it’s

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:01.250
a very special place.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:05.060
And there is something about (not to get into
too much of a history lesson on Newfoundland)

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:08.890
but our ancestors through the years having
to live off the land and the brutal climate,

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:13.780
that sort of helped inform who we are.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:23.020
And there is a dark, raw beauty to Newfoundland
and there is a strong independence and you

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:31.120
see that in our film as much as you see it
influenced from the words that were on the

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:32.120
script.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:35.080
What were your biggest challenges?

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:41.009
Matt Wells: So our biggest challenges (I mean
like most independent films) it’s budgetary,

00:07:41.009 --> 00:07:42.009
right?

00:07:42.009 --> 00:07:46.530
We only had so much money and we had to work
around that.

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:53.550
But honestly our biggest challenges on CROWN
AND ANCHOR came later as we went to market

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:56.510
and they were all self-inflicted wounds.

00:07:56.510 --> 00:08:02.780
Like I said everybody told us after reading
the script that they really thought it was

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:08.729
strong but we should not make a movie (as
first time filmmakers) that was running at

00:08:08.729 --> 00:08:15.340
two hours, that maybe some of the scenes were
too intense, maybe we shouldn’t score it

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:20.090
with punk and hardcore music because that
might alienate some audiences and that being

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:25.219
in such a beautiful part of the world we should
focus on these exteriors and show more of

00:08:25.219 --> 00:08:26.560
the landscape.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:34.440
But we did the opposite, not because we are
stubborn or we don’t want to collaborate,

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:42.210
it’s because those choices we felt were
true to the characters and true to the story

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:45.420
and that’s what we wanted to do, right?

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:52.890
So we made a movie that runs just under two
hours, no A-list stars, all punk and hardcore

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:59.450
music but these are the things that have allowed
us to get an international sales agent, these

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:05.170
are things that are allowing us to have a
North American theatrical release as an independent

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:08.990
film and it was because it was authentic to
the story and authentic to the characters.

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:10.900
And that’s what we’re doing, right?

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:15.980
As independent filmmakers what we’re trying
to do is chase the authentic stories.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:22.750
And we knew all these choices were only going
to help the film but again it was because

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:25.210
we knew who our audience was.

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:32.500
Knowing who your audience is is so important
and you know I go back to this idea of the

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:38.900
do-it-yourself punk and hardcore spirit from
that music world that inspired us so much

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:45.251
that we took it to the independent filmmaking
process, the same way that we used that when

00:09:45.251 --> 00:09:50.000
we were in a band, the same way we thought
about DIY when we were in production is the

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.510
same way we’re rolling out distribution.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:56.660
You know as independent filmmakers we want
to see our film in theaters, right?

00:09:56.660 --> 00:10:00.940
But it’s not always something that we have
access to.

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:05.160
We didn’t want to go straight to DVD, we
didn’t want to go straight to VOD or streaming,

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:10.240
so we’ve taken the same do-it-yourself spirit
and we’ve partnered with independent movie

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.740
theaters across North America to do our own
theatrical.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:19.550
Every independent film journey is going to
be different but this has been our’s.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:24.180
And it’s because we stuck to our vision,
it’s because we knew our audience and we

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:28.240
were willing to do the work because we believed
in what we were doing.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:31.020
So I hope you come out to see us when our
film hits your city.

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:36.320
You can check out our website [CrownandAnchorFilm.com]
which is up on the screen here and you know

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:37.320
come out and talk to us.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:42.640
We’re going to do Q&amp;A’s in some of the
cities afterward with myself, our writer/director

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:47.501
Andrew Rowe, our lead actor Michael Rowe (who
is also a producer on this with me), we want

00:10:47.501 --> 00:10:52.480
to talk to you guys and hear your questions
and know what you’re doing and tell you

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.030
about filming in Newfoundland.

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:55.260
So that it.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:57.370
I hope you get to check out CROWN AND ANCHOR.

00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:00.840
It’s raining so I’m going to go inside.

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:01.390
Thanks for listening.

